 <h2>What changed</h2>
 <p>The Dutch implementation is enacted, not proposed. Staatsblad 2025-337 took effect on 9 April 2026. Until 31 December 2028, the diisocyanate limits are 10 micrograms NCO per cubic metre over the working period and 20 for short exposure. From 1 January 2029 they become 6 and 12. <!-- CHECK: 04-limits --> The transition gives employers time to change processes and controls; it does not postpone the current 10/20 limits.</p>
 <p>Government guidance tells employers to put lead and diisocyanate risks in the RI&amp;E, add suitable measures to its action plan, offer medical controls where required and give specific information to workers. <!-- CHECK: 04-employer-actions --> Those are employer duties. They establish work, not a budget for this entrant, and the employer remains responsible after buying a report.</p>
-<p>A separate, older rule creates the training record. REACH entry 74 has barred industrial or professional use above 0.1% diisocyanates since 24 August 2023 unless the employer or self-employed user ensures successful training before use. <!-- CHECK: 04-reach-duty --> Training must match the process: ventilated-booth spraying is intermediate, while open-air or high-energy spraying such as foam is advanced. The employer documents completion and renews training at least every five years. Supplier material must be available in the official language of the Member State. <!-- CHECK: 04-reach-detail --></p>
+<p>A separate, older rule creates the training record. REACH entry 74 has barred industrial or professional use at or above 0.1% diisocyanates since 24 August 2023 unless the employer or self-employed user ensures successful training before use. <!-- CHECK: 04-reach-duty --> Training must match the process: ventilated-booth spraying is intermediate, while open-air or high-energy spraying such as foam is advanced. The employer documents completion and renews training at least every five years. Supplier material must be available in the official language of the Member State. <!-- CHECK: 04-reach-detail --></p>
 <p>That makes a certificate audit necessary but cheap to copy. It is not the moat. The harder record is the site-specific exposure assessment. The Labour Inspectorate says a basic characterisation may use a model. Its result can close the assessment or lead to measurements, and the chosen approach and results must be recorded in a report. <!-- CHECK: 04-assessment-method --></p>
 <p>This tests the pitch's first kill fact. A quantitative model is accepted; measurement is not automatic. <!-- CHECK: 04-assessment-method --> The Inspectorate also says measurement may need several days because conditions vary, and that the assessment must be performed by someone at occupational-hygienist level. <!-- CHECK: 04-expert-gate --> The viable unit is therefore not “a pump for every crew.” It is expert triage first, measurement only where the method and uncertainty require it.</p>
 <p>Enforcement can create the buying event. In an earlier inspection programme covering composite businesses, inspectors found violations at 76% of 89 initial visits and 42% of 67 follow-ups. They issued warnings, requirements and seven fines; four later fines concerned failure to establish or assess exposure. <!-- CHECK: 04-enforcement --> Inspectors also recorded firms seeking expert help after inspection. <!-- CHECK: 04-enforcement-response --> This is adjacent evidence, not a 2026 survey of spray contractors. It shows enforcement changing behaviour, but it shows no invoice or willingness to pay the proposed price.</p>
-<p>The pitch's number to settle first cannot be settled from the public record. In 2020 the Health Council said there was no register of PUR-foam installers and cited only a 2019 estimate of 100 to 200 individual workers. <!-- CHECK: 04-count-history --> The current NVPU member page lists two organisations under “Sprayers,” but it is a membership directory, not a census. <!-- CHECK: 04-nvpu-directory --> Vehicle repair, industrial coating and other spray users sit in different activity codes, while REACH requires each employer to keep its own completions. No public source found here reports either the national firm total or the share with valid certificates. <!-- CHECK: 04-count-boundary --></p>
+<p>The pitch's number to settle first cannot be settled from the public record. In 2020 the Health Council said there was no register of PUR-foam installers and cited only a 2019 estimate of 100 to 200 individual workers. <!-- CHECK: 04-count-history --> The current NVPU member page lists two organisations under “Sprayers,” but it is a membership directory, not a census. <!-- CHECK: 04-nvpu-directory --> Those two views do not aggregate vehicle repair, industrial coating or other spray users. REACH instead puts completion records with each employer. <!-- CHECK: 04-reach-detail --> No public source found here reports either the national firm total or the share with valid certificates. <!-- CHECK: 04-count-boundary --></p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="whyyou">
@@ -50,7 +50,7 @@
 <p>Polish and Russian help where the assessment depends on what workers actually do: how they mix, spray, clean, remove protection and respond to a blocked hose. The reader's CEE network can reach subcontracted crews and foreign-owned shops that a national provider may serve awkwardly. Those are working hypotheses from the edge inventory, not evidence that most Dutch spray crews speak these languages.</p>
 <p>The Dutch gate is real twice. First, the employer, RI&amp;E owner and Inspectorate operate in Dutch. A2 is not enough to discover commercial ambiguity or defend a technical report. Second, the Inspectorate expects occupational-hygienist-level expertise. <!-- CHECK: 04-expert-gate --> Its page names a competence level, not a statutory licence or an automatic right for this reader to sign.</p>
